              <section>
                <h3 class="font-bold text-fg">How the classifier works</h3>
                <p class="text-muted">I kept the notebook pipeline small enough to reason about in hardware. It loads the PNGs for each shape class and resizes them to 16×16 with OpenCV. Then it converts them to grayscale and flattens each one to a 256-value vector. Training is one-vs-all. You pick a <code>target_shape</code> (circle, square, triangle, or star), set its labels to 1 and all others to 0, and fit <code>SVC(kernel='linear')</code> from scikit-learn.</p>
                <p class="text-muted">Inference is one decision: <code>dot(w, pixels) + b &gt; 0</code>. A webcam frame goes through the same resize, and a threshold at 100 turns it into a black-and-white image. The same dot product then gives the answer: "This image is a square :)" or not.</p>
              </section>

              <section>
                <h3 class="font-bold text-fg">Quantizing the weights for hardware</h3>
                <p class="text-muted">Float weights are cheap in Colab and expensive on a chip. I scaled the learned weights and bias by 50 and rounded them to integers. Then I clipped the weights to the 4-bit range of −8 to 7, the range the notebook calls out. On the 3,000-image test set, the square detector scored 98.77% with float weights and 98.37% with only the integer <code>w_int</code> and <code>b_int</code>.</p>
              </section>

